// §3.6.1.1 derived natures: `nature ch3_child_n : ch3_parent_n; endnature` is
// the LRM's own "alias" shape — a derived nature with no items of its own, so
// every attribute comes from the parent.
//
// §3.6.1.2, access: "It is illegal for a derived nature to change the access
// attribute; the derived nature always inherits the access attribute of its
// parent nature." So the potential access function of a net whose discipline
// binds ch3_child_n is spelled ParentV, not some name derived from the child.
// §3.13.3: "Each access function is used with the name of the net as its
// argument", and it reads that net's potential.
//
// The testbench holds node p at 0.75, a value exact in binary, so the probe has
// exactly one right answer: ParentV(p) = 0.75. That fails if the child does not
// inherit ParentV (the module would not compile), and it fails if ParentV is
// bound to anything but p's potential.
//
// `p` is an `output` and not an `inout`: ch3_child_d binds a potential nature
// only, so it is a §1.3.4.1 signal-flow net, and that clause confines one to an
// `input` or an `output` port (E0360). `output` rather than `input` because the
// module drives it — contributing to an `input` signal-flow port is E0425, the
// sibling rule — and the `//! bias` is still the host's word on what the terminal
// is connected to, which is all this fixture needs of it.
//
//! lrm 3.6.1.2
//! lrm 3.6.1.1
//! lrm 1.3.4.1
//! bias V(p) = 0.75
`include "check.vh"
nature ch3_parent_n; units="V"; access=ParentV; abstol=1u; endnature
nature ch3_child_n:ch3_parent_n; endnature
discipline ch3_child_d; potential ch3_child_n; enddiscipline
module ch3_derived(p);
  output p;
  ch3_child_d p;
  analog begin
    `CHECKX("inherited access function probes p", ParentV(p), 0.75);
    ParentV(p) <+ 0.0;
  end
endmodule
